# Code owners for mctl-gitops.
#
# GitHub applies the LAST matching pattern, so the specific entries below
# override the repository-wide default rather than adding to it.
#
# This file ROUTES review; it does not require it. `main-protection` has
# `require_code_owner_review: false`, deliberately: mashkovd is the only
# account with write access to this repository, and GitHub does not count a
# pull request author's own review. Turning enforcement on would mean the
# only person who can open a status-flip PR is also the only person who
# cannot approve one, so every such PR would need `gh pr merge --admin`. A
# gate that is bypassed every time it fires teaches the bypass; it does not
# add a reviewer.
#
# What it does today is put matching pull requests in the owner's review
# queue automatically — for anyone other than the author. What it does later
# is start gating for real the moment a second account has write access,
# with no edit to this file. Revisit `require_code_owner_review` then.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow. This file is not an attempt to put a
# reviewer on everything in a repository that machines write to all day —
# it exists for the one class of change where "a machine wrote it" and "a
# human decided it" are indistinguishable from the diff alone.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Repository default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything, unless a more specific rule below says otherwise. Keep this
# line first: removing it would silently drop ownership of every path this
# file does not name.
* @MashkovD

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Proposal status files
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.status.yaml` is the file the Tier 2 implementer reads to decide whether a
# proposal is runnable. `status: accepted` plus an `approval` block naming a
# person is what authorises an agent to open a PR against a service repo, so
# an edit to this file is an authorisation decision wearing the clothes of a
# configuration change (gitops#986).
#
# The repository-wide rule above already routes these files; this entry does
# not add a reviewer that was missing. What it adds is the reason, at the
# path it applies to, so that narrowing the default later does not quietly
# take the proposal status files with it — and so that the one question
# worth asking here has a written answer.
#
# It reaches ONLY the human path, and that is the point:
#
#   - The agents write these files by pushing directly to main with
#     `mctl-gitops-deploy-key`, and DeployKey is a bypass actor on the
#     `main-protection` ruleset. Every `chore(agents): …` commit reaches main
#     without a pull request, so nothing here slows the loop down.
#   - A person cannot push to main. The ruleset requires a pull request, so a
#     hand-edited status flip — the shape of PR #1022, an operator restoring
#     a proposal to `accepted` after a quota failure — is exactly and only
#     what this line catches.
#
# What it does not cover: anyone holding the deploy key writes as the
# machines do, unreviewed. That is the ruleset's DeployKey bypass, not
# something a CODEOWNERS entry can reach.
platform-gitops/agents-state/*/proposals/*/.status.yaml @mashkovd

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agent platform catalog
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ExecutionProfiles, release bindings, the catalog policy and their schemas.
# A release binding is what lets a released agent definition be promoted to
# production (mctl-agents `tools/publish_agent_release.py` refuses a
# definition whose binding here is missing or does not match), and a profile
# states what an agent may do. A change under this path is therefore a
# decision about which agent exists and with what authority, not a
# configuration tweak (mctl-agents#470).
#
# As with the proposal status files above, the repository default already
# routes this path. The entry records the reason at the path it applies to,
# so that narrowing the default later does not drop it.
#
# What it does not do: the `main-protection` ruleset does not require a code
# owner's review, so this line names the owner without making the review
# mandatory, and a bypass actor on the ruleset is not reached by it at all.
# Both are ruleset matters, tracked in mctl-agents#470.
platform-gitops/agent-platform/** @mashkovd
